      "text": "To find the greatest common factor (GCF) of 40 and 48, we can use the Euclidean algorithm. The Euclidean algorithm involves repeated division and taking remainders until we reach a remainder of 0. The last non-zero remainder is the GCF.\n\n1. Divide 48 by 40:\n   \\[\n   48 \\div 40 = 1 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 48 - 40 \\cdot 1 = 8.\n   \\]\n   So, we can write:\n   \\[\n   48 = 40 \\cdot 1 + 8.\n   \\]\n\n2. Now, divide 40 by 8:\n   \\[\n   40 \\div 8 = 5 \\quad \\text{with a remainder of} \\quad 40 - 8 \\cdot 5 = 0.\n   \\]\n   So, we can write:\n   \\[\n   40 = 8 \\cdot 5 + 0.\n   \\]\n\nSince the remainder is now 0, the last non-zero remainder is 8. Therefore, the greatest common factor of 40 and 48 is \\(\\boxed{8}\\).",
